# plugin-drift-escalation.layer-a
# Untrusted plugin attempting the classic argument-drift confused-deputy (C2,
# blocked at verification).
#
# Intent: a malicious plugin tries to escalate by argument drift. It DECLARES,
# through its authored EffectDecl, that it writes to its own benign path
# /plugins/A/state.json -- the value a policy auditor or a capability check would
# inspect -- while the value it actually hands the foreign Fs.Write call is a
# sensitive host path, /etc/shadow. In a conventional host the privileged file
# writer would be tricked into using its authority on the attacker-chosen target:
# the checked value and the consumed value diverge. This is exactly the gap
# Q-039 closed. The effect projection on the prelude/authored Fs.Write binding
# pins the Filesystem.Write refinement to the call's argument 0 BY CONSTRUCTION,
# so the verifier requires the authored EffectDecl's parameter to be the SAME
# node as arguments[0]. They differ here (declaredPath vs actualPath), so the
# program is rejected at admission with ProjectionMismatch -- before any
# execution. The checked value cannot diverge from the value the foreign code
# receives.
@v=1 root=writeApp
strT PRM String
bytesT PRM Bytes
writeFx EFC "Filesystem.Write" [strT]
writeT FNT [strT bytesT] intT
writeFn FN "strand-builtin:Fs.Write" writeT [writeFx] "writeFx:0"
declaredPath STR "/plugins/A/state.json"
actualPath STR "/etc/shadow"
payload BYT "6d616c6963696f7573"
authoredDecl EFD writeFx [declaredPath]
writeApp APP writeFn [actualPath payload] [] [authoredDecl]
